#edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283966 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283966 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186283966 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "109ae18e-4733-5298-895a-59e0b555d56b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186282438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284072 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "543918ac-60ea-556f-87e9-9fe723e51c77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284525 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186283966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284525 #edb/ref 1069 "q-164833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284525 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284525 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{5}\\,,\\,{t}^{2}-2⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284525 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "854e79dd-e49f-5d57-896b-a1669e5ffce7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284525 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{5})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨10{t}^{4}\\,,\\,2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 10{t}^{4}+(-5)\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 10{t}^{4}-10t & =0 \\\\ 10t({t}^{3}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{5}\\,,\\,(1)^{2}-2⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-5⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2\\,,\\,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284532 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284532 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284532 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284532 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{5}-8,2{t}^{4}-6⟩,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284532 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a127c57-e392-5180-8b85-86041bead0c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284532 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{4}-6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =c⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5{t}^{4},8{t}^{3}⟩ & =⟨c,\\frac{4c}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{4}=c \\\\ 8{t}^{3}=\\frac{4c}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=5{t}^{4}.$ Substituting $c=5{t}^{4}$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{t}^{3} & =\\frac{4(5{t}^{4})}{5} \\\\ 8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4} & =0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3}(2-t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=2.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨(2)^{5}-8,2(2)^{4}-6⟩=⟨24,26⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1,\\frac{4}{5}⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(24,26).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3bdb4010-1e08-5378-a110-856c1b0d31e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343418 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8844755e-17ca-5997-8087-0445418bac62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343570 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343570 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343570 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "97e8fdd1-1609-5108-82df-e53e59510619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344097 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,-1,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344097 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344097 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d3b6012c-303d-5b23-b10c-010c9c93ca49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344464 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344464 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344464 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd335b51-418d-5e75-99ad-992ab400d4c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186342732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344833 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c171024e-4b6d-5601-9cbd-861fac1471b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345046 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a928b0be-2c4b-5a25-9350-6790b8cc0365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345046 #edb/ref 1074 "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
